title: Why the WebView does not use DMA-BUF
tags: [[GUI configuration]] [[Media preview]]
kind: rationale
audience: dev
aliases: dmabuf flicker WEBKIT_DISABLE_DMABUF_RENDERER webview-dmabuf
summary: With a video on screen, WebKitGTK's DMA-BUF path sometimes showed an old frame in turn with the current one; the GUI sets WEBKIT_DISABLE_DMABUF_RENDERER unless `webview-dmabuf` is turned on.

!! The symptom

During a slideshow of videos ([[Slideshow]]), moved through with the arrows, the
cursor of the [[metarecord-list panel]] started jumping between two rows, fast and
without end, while the video went on playing undisturbed — and, at other times, the
whole window flickered. Nothing else moved: the file panel kept its file, the
selection its metarecord.

!! What it was not

A probe patched into the page through WebKit's remote inspector recorded every
`cursor` class added and every redraw of the list's rows, with its call stack. During
the flicker it recorded ''nothing'': the page did not change. What alternated was what
reached the screen — the current frame and an older one, where the cursor still sat
on the previous row. No panel code can cause that, nor be fixed for it; and a test that
reads the DOM (`scripts/dev/gui-e2e.sh`) cannot see it, which is why it did not
reproduce there.

!! The setting

WebKitGTK hands the frames it composites — and it composites as soon as a video is on
the page — to the window as DMA-BUF buffers. With that path off
(`WEBKIT_DISABLE_DMABUF_RENDERER=1`, frames then go through shared memory) the
flicker was not seen again. So the GUI sets that variable at startup, before the
WebView exists, unless:

* `webview-dmabuf = true` in `[settings]` ([[GUI configuration]]) — the machine renders fine through DMA-BUF and wants it back;
* the variable is already set, whatever its value — set by hand for one run, it wins.

The cost is a copy per frame where DMA-BUF would share it, negligible without a GPU
(the container this was seen in has none), and small beside a GUI's frame rate
with one. A bug intermittent enough that "not seen again" is the evidence: if it
comes back with DMA-BUF off, `WEBKIT_DISABLE_COMPOSITING_MODE=1` is the next
suspect, with a heavier cost to video.
